ZTEXT0010000172e5e22971a0000000
ZERO NATIVE PROJECT BUILDER

tools/build.zero is the complete original Zero implementation. The native
zero-build or zero-build.exe program does not launch a shell, another process,
an external compiler, assembler, linker or language runtime. It uses the own
Zero compiler application and original image/ISO generators in process.

Usage:
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY [KERNEL_SOURCE]

The project supplies bootstrap/zero64.zapp, its own position-independent x64
compiler seed. The builder validates its ZEROAPP1 header and gives each call
a separate bounded stack, input and output. It compiles compiler.zero twice
to app64 and requires byte-for-byte equality of those two generations before
using that compiler for the system. Generated application mappings change
from RW to RX before execution. No RWX allocation is requested.

The optional third argument names an already composed kernel source. Without
it, PROJECT_DIRECTORY/kernel.zero is used. Kernel source may contain up to
524288 bytes: original Zero code compacts whitespace/comments outside quoted
strings, preserving separators and every quoted byte, then enforces the own
compiler's 524288-byte input limit. This is source composition, not translation
to another programming language. Kernel binary slot bounds remain explicit.

The complete build produces 78 artifacts in 82 stages: the two native compiler targets, BIOS, IA32/AMD64
UEFI loaders, the folder loader, both kernels, the six guest compiler apps,
the compiler bundle, and a real BIOS/UEFI optical zero.iso. It also builds
native Linux/Windows variants of the build, composition, checking, compiler
CLI, installer, updater, SHA256, text-container, picture and archive tools,
plus Zero Studio and the own web-document reader. The0.14media addition builds
Linux/Windows editions of zero-media, zero-flash, zero-drives, zero-disk and
zero-media-check, the disk BIOS stages, both UEFI flash writers, both UEFI
media menus, and the media FAT generator. The0.15preview adds native Linux and
Windows zero-web, zero-web-compose and zero-web-check. This new local browser
engine is separate from the existing guest HTTP reader; the OS image itself
has not acquired full CSS/JavaScript or HTTPS support. Source files are never
overwritten. See browser/engine/README.ztext and VERIFICATION.ztext.

The builder emits the components and tools for the custom disk format. To
produce a distributable .zmedia after the build, use its own zero-disk with
the build directory and its disk-bootstrap.bin/disk-bios.bin paths, then
zero-media make DISK_PAYLOAD NEW.zmedia. See media/README.ztext. The flash
program validates ZMEDIA01 and writes its whole-disk payload; this container
is distinct from the compatibility zero.iso optical image.
App/compiler output limits, ELF/PE targets and firmware constraints are the
same as the normal Zero compiler. The ISO embeds the original image builders'
FAT16 payload. Padded slots and that intermediate filesystem image exist only
in owned memory; no .img or other intermediate disk image is written.

Current layout: kernel64 slot 475136 bytes, kernel32 slot 507904 bytes; together
they remain 983040 bytes. The 1 MiB bundle contains eight application records:
six original compiler/frontend/packer images and two HTML reader images.
Original app offsets are unchanged; each packer now has a 16384-byte slot.
The remaining 143360 bytes of each former packer slot hold the matching reader
at offsets339968 and819200. Compiler/frontend slots remain159744 bytes.
The runtime prefix/body slots remain 4096/65536 bytes. The final ISO is 18890752
bytes. The original builders validate their own generated container inputs.

The output directory must not exist. Each completed artifact is created
exclusively, fully written and filesync'd. A failing current write removes
only its own partial artifact. Earlier completed artifacts remain in the new
directory for inspection; a nonzero exit means the directory is incomplete.
Existing directories, files, source and user data disks are never replaced.
File flush does not establish directory-metadata power-loss durability.

Build the builder from included source with our packaged native compiler CLI:
  zero tools/build.zero zero-build
On Windows use zero.exe and an output name ending in .exe. The release can
also provide the already compiled own builder in bootstrap/.
The raw compiler named zero in build output accepts source on standard input;
the packaged zero/ezero command includes the separate companion lib directory.

Native source regeneration and checking are separate own programs:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_FRONTEND.zero --ezero
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_PICTURE.zero --picture
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_PICTURE_CHECK.zero --picture-check
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_READER.zero --browser
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_READER_CHECK.zero --browser-check
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
See tools/integrate.ztext and checks/README.ztext for their exact contracts.

Memory use is a 64 MiB native allocation plus the native process stack. Inputs
must come from a trusted original project: executing a compiler seed and
compiling arbitrary unsafe Zero source is not a sandbox. Do not edit inputs
concurrently during a build. Bounded reads detect size changes and short I/O;
they do not provide an atomic snapshot of the entire project directory.

0.16 addition: Linux and Windows zero-inspect, zero-inspect-check and
zero-inspect-compose. The inspector reads at most 4096 header bytes and
never runs or extracts input. It reports header consistency separately
from content integrity. See tools/inspect.ztext. Zero Web adds --help,
--capabilities and a built-in --about page through the existing renderer.
See browser/engine/CAPABILITIES.ztext. Boot and guest code are unchanged.
